# Task Register

> This file is the **single source of truth** for task status, and the only table a team reconciles against: who is on what, how far it got, and why it is stuck are recorded here and nowhere else.
> Which rows are in play this round comes from the session instruction, so no separate task-list file exists.
> Conventions live in `CLAUDE.md` under "Task Tracking", the playbook in [workflow](rules/workflow.md). Replace every `{{PLACEHOLDER}}` and run `python3 scripts/check-project.py` before use.

## Conventions

- One task per row; an ID never changes once assigned. Edit the original row when item, status, dependency, or owner changes — never append an "Update:" section.
- **One row, one owner.** Put a single name in `Assignee` so responsibility is personal. When several people push the same goal, split it into subtasks that each own a row — never write "A+B" or "the team".
- **Commit right after editing.** Commit only the rows and bodies you touched; inside a single repository git merges different rows automatically. Do not re-sort the table or re-pad columns "for tidiness" — that turns everyone's edits into conflicts.
- On handover, change `Assignee` and state in Notes "how far it got and how to restart", without opening a new task or copying the body.
- Break large stages into a subtask chain: parent `S1`, children `S1.1`, `S1.2`; each child states action, artifact, and closing condition.
- Status has exactly four values: `active` (in progress), `blocked` (dependency unmet), `done` (closed), `superseded` (retired).
- Move to `done` only when the evidence exists and the closing condition is met. Deferred, dependency-unmet, and not-enabled are not done.
- **Status appears once, in this table.** Express the current stage as a group heading and put blockers and next actions in the Notes column of `blocked` rows; no body document may keep a focus table, an archive ledger, or a second status table — they link to IDs only.
- Point each body document's `owner_task` front-matter field at an ID here; a retired body declares `superseded_by` and its task is marked `superseded`.
- History belongs to git; this table only shows the present. No process log, no dated "updated" bullets.
- Commit, push, merge, build, and deploy are separately authorized steps: listing them is not permission to run them.

## Columns

| Column | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| ID | Stable task ID; starts with a letter, may contain digits, dots, underscores, e.g. `S1`, `S1.1` |
| Item | One sentence on what is being delivered |
| Status | `active` / `blocked` / `done` / `superseded` |
| Depends on | Upstream task IDs; `—` when none |
| Assignee | The single accountable person; `unassigned` until someone claims it, and nobody else edits that row |
| Closing condition | A verifiable artifact or acceptance boundary, never "take a look at it" |
| Body | Relative link to the plan, steps, and acceptance notes under `.project/`; `—` when absent |
| Evidence | Relative link to machine evidence, logs, or verification records; `—` when not produced |
| Updated | `YYYY-MM-DD`; bump it on every status change |
| Notes | Blocker, scope, handover point, next action; `—` when empty |

